In that text, 'Paṭiccasamuppāda' means the mode of conditions (paccayākāra). Indeed, the mode of conditions brings about states that arise together by depending on one another. Therefore, it is called 'Paṭiccasamuppāda' (Dependent Origination). This is a brief explanation here; the detailed explanation should be taken from the Visuddhimagga.

Regarding 'vo': This word 'vo' is seen in the senses of the nominative (paccatta), accusative (upayoga), instrumental (karaṇa), dative (sampadāna), genitive (sāmivacana), and as a verse-filler (padapūraṇa). In passages such as "Anuruddhas, are you (vo) united and rejoicing?", it is seen in the nominative sense. In passages such as "Monks, go, I dismiss you (vo)", it is seen in the accusative sense. In passages such as "You (vo) should not dwell in my presence", it is seen in the instrumental sense. In passages such as "Monks, I will preach the Vanapattha discourse to you (vo)", it is seen in the dative sense. In passages such as "Sāriputta, the speech of all of you (vo) is well-spoken", it is seen in the genitive sense. In passages such as "Those noble ones (vo) who are of pure bodily action", it is seen as a mere verse-filler. Here, however, this word 'vo' should be understood in the dative sense. 'Bhikkhave' is the addressing again of the monks who have turned towards Him in acknowledgment. 'Desessāmi' is the promise to teach the discourse. 'Taṃ suṇātha' means 'listen to that discourse on Dependent Origination being spoken by me'.

Regarding 'sādhukaṃ manasi karotha': Here, the two words 'sādhukaṃ' and 'sādhu' have the same meaning. This word 'sādhu' is seen in the senses of requesting (āyācana), acceptance (sampaṭicchana), gladdening (sampahaṃsana), goodness (sundara), and making firm (daḷhīkamma), etc. In passages such as "Venerable Sir, may the Blessed One teach me the Dhamma in brief (sādhu)", it is seen in the sense of requesting. In passages such as "'Very well (sādhu), Venerable Sir,' that monk, having rejoiced and appreciated the words of the Blessed One", it is seen in the sense of acceptance. In passages such as "Excellent, excellent (sādhu sādhu), Sāriputta", it is seen in the sense of gladdening.

"A king who delights in Dhamma is good (sādhu);
A wise man is good (sādhu);
Non-betrayal of friends is good (sādhu);
Not doing evil is happiness."

In such passages, it is seen in the sense of goodness. In passages such as "Brahmin, therefore listen well (sādhukaṃ)", the word 'sādhukaṃ' itself is seen in the sense of making firm; it is also said to be in the sense of a command (āṇatti). Here, however, this word 'sādhukaṃ' should be understood in the senses of making firm and command; it is also appropriate in the sense of goodness. By the sense of making firm, it is intended to show: "Listen to this Dhamma firmly, grasping it well." By the sense of command, it is intended to show: "Listen according to my command." By the sense of goodness, it is intended to show: "Listen to this good and excellent Dhamma." 'Manasi karotha' means 'reflect upon it'.
